<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Madison</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Madison was the fourth president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, from 1809 to 1817, and a principal author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">US Constitution</a> and the Bill of Rights. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a> and John Jay he wrote The Federalist essays in defence of the Constitution. As president he annexed part of Spanish West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in 1810 and led the country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-madison.jpg" alt="James Madison" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Vanderlyn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Madison.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James Madison</b> discussed the Achaean League in The Federalist No.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In America, Thomas Jefferson drafted the United States Declaration of Independence of 1776, and <b>James Madison</b> took a leading part in framing the constitution of 1787.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>9 (1787) Alexander Hamilton wrote that “it is impossible to read the history of the petty republics of Greece and Italy without feeling sensations of horror and disgust at the distractions with which they were continually agitated”, and <b>James Madison</b> argued in No.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James Madison</b>, a framer of the Constitution of the United States, wrote in Federalist No.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James Madison</b> called the American Constitution “neither a national nor a federal Constitution, but a composition of both”.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James Madison</b> explained the separation of powers by human nature: since men were not angels, government had to be made to control itself, and “Ambition must be made to counteract ambition.”</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Port Conway, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Montpelier, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.18,-77.19&amp;year=1806" aria-label="Port Conway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2628 792 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Madison"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3078 1062h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3078 1062h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.18,-77.19&amp;year=1806">What was here in 1806? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But what is government itself, but the greatest of all reflections on human nature? If men were angels, no government would be necessary. If angels were to govern men, neither external nor internal controls on government would be necessary.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 51 (1788) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/fed51.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In framing a government which is to be administered by men over men, the great difficulty lies in this: you must first enable the government to control the governed; and in the next place oblige it to control itself.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 51 (1788) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/fed51.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It could never be more truly said than of the first remedy, that it was worse than the disease. Liberty is to faction what air is to fire, an aliment without which it instantly expires.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 10 (1787) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/18th_century/fed10.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Justice is the end of government. It is the end of civil society. It ever has been and ever will be pursued until it be obtained, or until liberty be lost in the pursuit.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 51 (1788) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1404/pg1404-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is in vain to say that enlightened statesmen will be able to adjust these clashing interests, and render them all subservient to the public good. Enlightened statesmen will not always be at the helm.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 10 (1787) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1404/pg1404-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The accumulation of all powers, legislative, executive, and judiciary, in the same hands, whether of one, a few, or many, and whether hereditary, self-appointed, or elective, may justly be pronounced the very definition of tyranny.&#8221;</p><footer>The Federalist No. 47 (1788) &#183; original &#183; published under the name Publius &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1404/pg1404-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
